{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>וַיֵּצֵא בֶּן אִשָּׁה וגו׳.</b> It says <b>וַיֵּצֵא</b> after the manner that our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Tanchuma) regarding the verse ״וַיֵּצֵא הָעֵגֶל הַזֶּה״ — that it went out without the intention of the one who made it. So too here: the Israelite woman did not intend the act of seeding, but the seed came forth without intention for a forbidden matter, as our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Exodus Rabbah 1:28) that the Egyptian master set her husband to his labor and returned to the house and committed the act, and the woman thought he was her husband, and this seed resulted. And therefore you will find that Scripture equates the mention of the woman to the mention of the Israelite man, for it is written <b>הָאִשָּׁה הַיִּשְׂרְאֵלִית וְאִישׁ הַיִּשְׂרְאֵלִי</b> — for the woman is innocent of sin and equal to the Israelite man mentioned before us, in whom there is no blemish. And it says <b>בְּתוֹךְ בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל</b> to say that there was among them no mamzer like him." "<b>Further,</b> it intends that they did not give him a dwelling place among them — for in any place where he wished to pitch his tent they would say to him, ״You are not one of us.״ And our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Torat Kohanim) that Scripture comes to say that he converted, and both these and those are words of the living Elohim." "<b>וְאִישׁ הַיִּשְׂרְאֵלִי.</b> The reason his name was not mentioned is perhaps that He did not wish to mention him, because through him the matter came about that the son of the Israelite woman blasphemed the Name, and ״one thing brings about another,״ and so on, and the Holy One, blessed be He, does not desire to shame a person — all the more so in the Torah, whose impression endures forever and ever." "<b>Further,</b> Scripture intends to say that the dispute over which they fought was concerning the distinction noted — that this one is the son of an Israelite woman and this one is the son of an Israelite man. And here Scripture included everything our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Leviticus Rabbah 32:3) — according to the one who says it was about lineage, and according to the one who says, and so on. And Scripture did not bother to elaborate and also state his name, for there is no need for that."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:00:46.144235599Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}